.Dd June 4, 2026
.Dt XTC_AIO 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_aio_pread ,
.Nm xtc_aio_pwrite ,
.Nm xtc_aio_fsync ,
.Nm xtc_aio_fdatasync
.Nd async file I/O for fibers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_aio.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_aio_pread "int fd" "void *buf" "uint32_t len" "int64_t off"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_aio_pwrite "int fd" "const void *buf" "uint32_t len" "int64_t off"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_aio_fsync "int fd"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_aio_fdatasync "int fd"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These perform a file read, write, or data-sync that suspends the
calling fiber and resumes it on completion, keeping the fiber's loop
live for other work while the I/O is outstanding.  They use the
platform's best mechanism, chosen at run time:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
On a loop whose I/O backend is io_uring, the operation is submitted
natively
.Pq Dv IORING_OP_READ , IORING_OP_WRITE , No or Dv IORING_OP_FSYNC ;
the fiber parks and the kernel's completion re-enqueues it.  No worker
thread is used.
.It
On a readiness-only backend
.Pq epoll, kqueue, poll, select
the operation is offloaded to the blocking thread pool
.Pq Xr xtc_blocking 3 ,
because a regular file is not pollable -- io_uring is the only POSIX
backend that can complete file I/O asynchronously, and a thread offload
is the correct fallback elsewhere.
.It
Off a loop entirely
.Pq no current fiber
the operation runs inline on the calling thread.
.El
.Pp
This is the layer that lets storage code -- a buffer manager paging the
B-tree, a WAL writer -- use the fastest async file path a host offers
without the caller choosing or knowing which one.  In every case the
call returns only once the operation has completed (or failed), so the
caller may reuse
.Fa buf
immediately afterward.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_aio_pread
reads up to
.Fa len
bytes from
.Fa fd
at absolute offset
.Fa off
into
.Fa buf ;
.Fn xtc_aio_pwrite
writes
.Fa len
bytes from
.Fa buf
at
.Fa off ;
.Fn xtc_aio_fsync
flushes the file's data and metadata durably;
.Fn xtc_aio_fdatasync
flushes the data only
.Pq fdatasync semantics; the hot path for page and WAL flushes, and what
.Xr xtc_io 3 Ns 's storage callers use .
On macOS, which has no
.Fn fdatasync ,
the data-only call falls back to
.Fn fsync .
None of them moves the file's seek pointer.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_aio_pread
and
.Fn xtc_aio_pwrite
return the number of bytes transferred
.Pq Ar >= 0 ;
a short read at end of file returns the partial count, not an error.
.Fn xtc_aio_fsync
returns 0.
On failure each returns a negative
.Va errno
value
.Pq e.g. Li -EIO .
.Sh CONTEXT
Call from a fiber running on a loop for the async path; off a loop the
operation runs synchronously on the calling thread.  The buffer must
remain valid until the call returns, which is guaranteed simply by not
returning from the call -- the fiber parks on this one operation's
completion and arms no other waker, so the operation is never left in
flight referencing a stack buffer that has gone away.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Page a 4 KiB block out and read it back, off the fiber's loop:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int n = xtc_aio_pwrite(fd, page, 4096, (int64_t)pid * 4096);
if (n != 4096) return -1;
if (xtc_aio_fsync(fd) != 0) return -1;
n = xtc_aio_pread(fd, page, 4096, (int64_t)pid * 4096);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_io 3 ,
.Xr xtc_blocking 3 ,
.Xr xtc_loop 3
